ADS-013 — Versioning Model

Document ID: ADS-013
Title: Versioning Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Versioning Model (CVM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Versioning Model establishes standardized mechanisms for managing the evolution of Canonical Atlas Objects (CAOs) while preserving identity, provenance, traceability, compatibility, and engineering integrity.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Version architecture
Revision model
Baseline model
Branching model
Compatibility model
Supersession
Historical lineage
Version governance

This specification applies to every versioned Atlas object.

3. Canonical Definition

A Version is a uniquely identifiable state of a Canonical Atlas Object representing its content at a specific point in its lifecycle while preserving the object's persistent identity.

Object identity remains constant.

Versions evolve independently.

4. Objectives

The Versioning Model SHALL:

Preserve object identity.
Preserve engineering history.
Support controlled evolution.
Support traceability.
Support compatibility.
Enable rollback.
Enable reproducibility.
Preserve governance.
5. Versioning Principles

Every Atlas object SHALL follow these principles.

Persistent Identity

Object identity SHALL NOT change.

Immutable Versions

Published versions SHALL NOT be modified.

Explicit Evolution

Every change SHALL create a new version.

Complete Lineage

All versions SHALL remain connected.

Traceable Changes

Every version SHALL record why it exists.

Governance

Version creation SHALL follow governance policies.

6. Canonical Version Structure

Each version SHALL include:

Property	Description
Object ID	Persistent object identity
Version ID	Unique version identity
Version Number	Canonical version designation
Parent Version	Previous version
Baseline	Associated baseline
Status	Draft, Approved, etc.
Created Timestamp	Version creation
Change Summary	Description of changes
Author	Creator
Approval Status	Governance state
7. Version Categories

Atlas defines the following canonical version categories.

Category	Purpose
Draft	Working revision
Review	Under evaluation
Approved	Official version
Released	Available for operational use
Deprecated	Scheduled for retirement
Archived	Historical version
8. Semantic Versioning

Atlas adopts semantic versioning.

Major.Minor.Patch
Major

Represents incompatible semantic changes.

Example:

1.0.0

↓

2.0.0
Minor

Represents compatible functional extensions.

Example:

1.2.0

↓

1.3.0
Patch

Represents corrections without semantic change.

Example:

1.2.1

↓

1.2.2
9. Revision Model

Every revision SHALL include:

Revision identifier
Parent version
Revision reason
Engineering justification
Author
Timestamp
Approval reference

Revision history SHALL remain immutable.

10. Branching Model

Atlas supports controlled branching.

Version 1
      │
      ▼
Version 2
   │      │
   ▼      ▼
Branch A  Branch B
   │      │
   └──┬───┘
      ▼
Merged Version

Branches SHALL preserve complete lineage.

11. Baseline Model

A Baseline represents an approved collection of versions considered stable for a defined purpose.

Examples:

Project baseline
Design baseline
Regulatory baseline
Release baseline

Baselines SHALL reference immutable versions.

12. Compatibility Model

Atlas defines four compatibility classes.

Compatibility	Description
Fully Compatible	Interchangeable
Backward Compatible	Supports previous consumers
Forward Compatible	Supports future consumers
Incompatible	Requires migration

Compatibility SHALL be explicitly declared.

13. Supersession

Superseded versions SHALL:

Remain available.
Preserve references.
Record superseding version.
Preserve complete history.

Supersession SHALL NOT delete historical versions.

14. Lineage

Version lineage SHALL remain reconstructable.

Object

↓

Version 1

↓

Version 2

↓

Version 3

↓

Version 4

Branches SHALL preserve parent-child relationships.

15. Governance

Every version SHALL define:

Author
Reviewer
Approver
Approval timestamp
Change justification
Applicable policies
Audit references

Version governance SHALL remain immutable.

16. Quality Requirements

Every version SHALL satisfy:

Traceability
Integrity
Reproducibility
Consistency
Compatibility
Governance compliance
Explainability
Historical completeness
17. Conformance

A versioned Atlas object conforms if it:

Preserves persistent identity.
Creates immutable versions.
Maintains lineage.
Implements semantic versioning.
Records revision history.
Supports governance.
Supports compatibility declarations.
18. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Distributed version federation.
Collaborative engineering branches.
AI-assisted merge analysis.
Automated compatibility validation.
Temporal version queries.
Cross-organization baselines.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical version semantics.

Canonical Version Architecture
Canonical Atlas Object
          │
          ▼
Persistent Identity
          │
 ┌────────┼─────────────┐
 ▼        ▼             ▼
Version 1 Version 2 Version 3
     │         │
     ▼         ▼
 Branch A   Branch B
       └──────┬──────┘
              ▼
        Approved Baseline

The object's identity remains constant while versions evolve through controlled governance.